Composing the split with the deterministic isic -&gt; isic.3 aggregation can do one of two things to a combo code’s children: reconverge (every child lands back in the same isic.3 parent – the 1/n split weights cancel out exactly on re-aggregation, so the isic.3-level figure is mathematically exact regardless of the split), or cross (children land in different isic.3 parents – now the even-split assumption directly determines how much value each parent receives, with no reconvergence to cancel it out). Only crossing components carry real, propagated allocation uncertainty past the split step; reconverging ones are imputed at the 4-digit level but exact at 3-digit and coarser. This is exactly what the node-link diagrams in Explorer/Country variants show per component (dashed split edges into a reconvergent or diverging fan); crosses_isic3 above is that same distinction at the per-(country, year, isiccomb) grain.
Stage-1+2 coverage tile: frac_split_crossing is the same denominator as frac_split above (fraction of a country-year’s isic values), restricted to values whose combo group crosses an isic.3 boundary – i.e. a decomposition, frac_split = frac_split_reconverging + frac_split_crossing, isolating the fraction of “touched by imputation” that’s actually consequential once you account for the second stage:


